.\"
.\" FSYNC(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH FSYNC 2 "2026-09-29" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
fsync, fdatasync \- write a file's data to stable storage
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ,\~ \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.BI "int fsync(int " fd );
.BI "int fdatasync(int " fd );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fsync ()
writes the data and metadata of the file open on
.I fd
to the storage device and waits until the device reports them stable,
including any volatile write cache the device has.
When it returns 0, the file survives a crash or power failure.
.PP
.BR fdatasync ()
is the same as
.BR fsync ()
on Substrate.
POSIX lets it skip metadata not needed to read the data back; Substrate
writes everything.
.PP
Substrate's buffer cache does not record which buffers belong to which
file, so both calls do the work of
.BR sync (2):
every file system's deferred metadata, every dirty buffer, and then a
cache flush of every block device.
POSIX allows
.BR fsync ()
to write more than the one file.
.PP
To make a newly created file's directory entry durable, call
.BR fsync ()
on a descriptor for the directory as well (or rely on the fact that, on
Substrate, any
.BR fsync ()
writes all metadata).
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EBADF
.I fd
is not an open file descriptor.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I fd
refers to a pipe, FIFO, socket or kqueue, which cannot be synchronized.
.TP
.B EIO
Writing a buffer to its device failed, or a device reported that
flushing its write cache failed.
Because every device is flushed, this can report a failure on a device
other than the one holding the file.
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
Syscall Number: 527 (SYS_FSYNC)
Arguments:      int fd
Returns:        0, or \-errno
.fi
.PP
.BR fdatasync ()
has no system call of its own; the C library calls
.BR fsync ().
The FreeBSD and NetBSD personalities route their
.BR fsync
and
.BR fdatasync
system calls to the same kernel function.
.SH EXAMPLE
Write a file so that it survives a crash, then make its name durable:
.PP
.EX
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int
save(const char *path, const void *buf, size_t len)
{
    int fd = open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);

    if (fd < 0)
        return \-1;
    if (write(fd, buf, len) != (ssize_t)len || fsync(fd) != 0) {
        close(fd);
        return \-1;
    }
    return close(fd);
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sync (2),
.BR msync (2),
.BR open (2),
.BR write (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024.
